course: dictionary
lesson: 14725
title: Being proficient
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The words around a new word are its context, and they often give clues. «Sam rides safely through the busy town; he is a proficient cyclist.» Sam rides safely in traffic, so proficient must mean skilled at cycling. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check: "he is a skilled cyclist" still makes sense.
explain: Proficient often comes from practice. «After a year of lessons, Priya is proficient at playing the piano.» The clue is "after a year of lessons": she became good at it through training and practice.
explain: In school reports, proficient is a level, not a feeling. «Ben is proficient in reading, but still developing in maths.» Here proficient means Ben has reached the expected level in reading. Developing means he is still getting there.
fact: Proficient means good at something because of practice and training; skilled.
fact: In school reports, «proficient» means you have reached the expected level in a subject.
fact: Useful phrases: «proficient in», «proficient at», «highly proficient» and «become proficient».
fact: Other school levels are «developing» (still growing) and «advanced» (ahead of the expected level).
fact: Proficient is an adjective, so it describes a noun, as in «a proficient swimmer». Its comparative is «more proficient».
exercise: «Mia is a proficient swimmer.» What does proficient mean? | Slow and careful in the water | Tired after a long day | *Skilled, good at swimming because she has practised | Afraid of deep water
exercise: «The report said Ali was proficient in English.» In a school report, what does proficient mean? | *He has reached the expected level in English | He has never studied English | He has left the school | He is the only pupil who speaks English
exercise: «Sam rode safely through the busy town, as a proficient cyclist should.» Copy the word that means skilled. | town | cyclist | busy | *proficient
test: «After months of practice, Lena became proficient at the violin.» What does proficient mean here? | Bored of the violin | Lost in the music | Too young to play | *Skilled, because she practised a lot
test: Which word fits the gap? «The pupils must be ___ in English before they move up.» | loud | *proficient | sleepy | happy
exam: Which sentence uses proficient correctly? | The bus was proficient because it was red | *Priya became proficient at chess after a year of practice | The cake was proficient and tasted of soap | The rain was proficient all afternoon
